# Never ship configuration or secrets — the image is environment-agnostic and a
# deployment's integrations are configured in the setup wizard (ADR-041).
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.min.example.*

# Build output and caches: rebuilt inside the image, and copying host artifacts
# in would silently mix architectures.
node_modules
**/node_modules
.next
**/.next
dist
**/dist
.turbo
**/.turbo
coverage
**/coverage

# The Lambda deployment target is not part of any container image. It sits
# outside the pnpm workspace so its dependencies never reach the image; this
# keeps its source out too, since the Dockerfile copies the whole tree
# (ADR-056 §2). The DSS signature engine is the exception: its sources are
# built into the default image (ADR-069 §3), but never a host-built jar.
deploy/*
!deploy/dss
deploy/dss/target

# Repository and tooling noise that only slows the build context transfer.
.git
.github
.claude
docs
*.md
!README.md
test-results
playwright-report
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Written into the checkout by CI steps that run between builds: trivy-action
# keeps its multi-GB vulnerability DB in .cache/trivy and merges the ignore
# files into ./trivyignores, publish.yml writes the CycloneDX SBOM, and
# `docker compose up` creates ./trust as the empty source of
# docker-compose.prod.yml's bind mount. Left in the context, they change
# `COPY . .`, so the next build misses the cache, reruns `pnpm build` and ships
# the files in the image. ./trust is deployment config in any case — the signer
# CA certificates an operator mounts; the image creates /app/trust itself.
.cache
trivy_envs.txt
trivyignores
*.cdx.json
trust
